<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humbert I, Count of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1047</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Humbert White-Hands, Humbert the White-Handed · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Humbert I, known as White-Hands, was a noble of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> who held lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> and Savoy from about 1003. He gained control of Alpine passes and was rewarded by Emperor Conrad II for his support. He is regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, which later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Humbert I, Count of Savoy" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pelagio Palagi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comte_Humbert_I_de_Savoie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Humbert was the son of Amadeus, who may or may not have preceded him as count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a>. His brother was Bishop Otto of Belley. The origins of the family are unknown: his ancestors are variously said to have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> or Provence. Because Humbert had close connections with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">Rudolf III of Burgundy</a>, it is likely that his family was Burgundian, descended either from the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> or from an aristocratic family such as the Guigonids, ancestors of the counts of Albon. He was probably also related to Ermengarde of Burgundy, the second wife of Rudolf III. He first held lands around Belley and in the county of Sermorens, before gaining lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His service to the Holy Roman Emperors Henry II and Conrad II was rewarded with the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a> and lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, all at the expense of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and archbishops. After Rudolf III died in 1032, Humbert swore fealty to Conrad II. He supported the emperor in his campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-ii-of-blois">Odo II, Count of Blois</a>, and Aribert, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In return Conrad appointed him count of Savoy and granted him Maurienne, Chablais and perhaps Tarentaise. These grants to a loyal supporter secured key passes through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, which controlled trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and they formed the core of Savoy power for centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Humbert married a woman named Ancelie, also written <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">Auxilia</a> or Ancilia. She may have been the daughter of Anselme of Aoste, or of the master of ceremonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, or a member of a northern Italian dynasty known as the Anselmids. They had at least four sons. Amadeus I and Otto both became counts of Savoy in succession, Aymon was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Sion and Burchard was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. Humbert is often said to have died at Hermillon, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, held the county and expanded from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Humbert I of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The house of Savoy had ruled lands on both sides of the western Alps since the eleventh century, when <b>Humbert the White-Handed</b> held counties around the Lake of Geneva and the valleys leading to the Mont Cenis and Great St Bernard passes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Humbert White-Hands</b>, a noble of the Kingdom of Arles, held counties around Maurienne and Savoy from around 1003.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Hermillon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.3,6.36&amp;year=1037" aria-label="Hermillon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4701 565 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Humbert I, Count of Savoy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5151 835h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5151 835h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.3,6.36&amp;year=1037">What was here in 1037? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
